gh until February/March - so with some planning you’ll be eating home grown parsnips for your Christmas roast","image":"moja08899.jpg","commonName":"parsnip","nurseriesCount":4,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":true,"price":null},{"id":293182,"botanicalName":"<em>Pastinaca</em> <em>sativa</em> 'Albion'","entityDescription":"This variety produces good yields of uniform long, smooth, white roots which taper evenly. The white skin is slow to discolour and the roots have a lovely sweet flavour with a good texture. Bred for resistance to canker so it is a good option for organic gardeners.  Sow: March to May, Harvest: September to February","image":"","commonName":"parsnip 'Albion'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":true,"price":null},{"id":173852,"botanicalName":"<em>Pastinaca</em> <em>sativa</em> 'Countess'","entityDescription":"A relatively new maincrop variety with good yields and lovely pale cream, sweet flesh. This variety is considered a good option for the show bench, particularly as it retains its colour after washing, but is equally good for the garden and allotment. The parsnips show good resistance to canker and can be harvested all through winter so you should be enjoying home grown parsnips for Christmas dinner!\r\nSow: March to May, Harvest: September to February.","image":"","commonName":"parsnip 'Countess'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":true,"price":null},{"id":139331,"botanicalName":"<em>Pastinaca</em> <em>sativa</em> 'Gladiator'","entityDescription":"An established leading variety, this is a top-quality parsnip, that is often grown by the exhibitors as it’s so reliable and trouble-free. The long, slender roots are a soft pale cream with consistently smooth, blemish-free skin and a rich, sweet and earthy flavour. It grows well in most soils, is resistant to canker and is generally very healthy. Plants are quick to mature and can produce a crop very early in the season. \r\nSow: Mid-March to mid-May, Harvest: September to February","image":"grjo01605.jpg","commonName":"parsnip 'Gladiator'","nurseriesCount":5,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":true,"price":null},{"id":173861,"botanicalName":"<em>Pastinaca</em> <em>sativa</em> 'Hollow Crown'","entityDescription":"Grown in Britain from at least 1820, this is one of the oldest varieties that is still being grown today. Although its growth habit is a bit untidy it more than makes up for it with its delicious taste and texture – it’s sweet and smooth so mashes and roasts well. It also has a good resistance to canker and stores well so it ticks all the boxes! The creamy white roots are long, between 25cm and 40cm, so prefer a light stone free soil. Sow: March to May, Harvest: September to February","image":"","commonName":"parsnip 'Hollow Crown'","nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":true,"price":null},{"id":222128,"botanicalName":"<em>Pastinaca</em> <em>sativa</em> 'Palace'<sup>PBR</sup>","entityDescription":"An excellent variety that produces smooth-skinned, wedge-shaped roots with a delicious, sweet taste. Growth is uniform and vigorous with some resistance to canker.  \r\nSow: February to May, Harvest: October to February","image":"","commonName":"parsnip 'Palace'","nurseriesCount":3,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":true,"price":null},{"id":173847,"botanicalName":"<em>Pastinaca</em> <em>sativa</em> 'Panache'<sup>PBR</sup>","entityDescription":"This smooth, white-skinned variety has creamy flesh with a sweet flavour. The roots are large and wedge shaped, have a good resistance to canker and store well.  Sow: April to June, Harvest: September to February","image":"","commonName":"parsnip 'Panache'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":true,"price":null},{"id":362434,"botanicalName":"<em>Pastinaca</em> <em>sativa</em> 'Pearl'<sup>PBR</sup>","entityDescription":"Attractive, long slender roots with a smooth white skin and a pleasant taste make this a good variety to grow. What makes it stand out though is that it stores brilliantly in the ground over winter and has good canker resistance. Sow: February to May, Harvest: July to March","image":"","commonName":"parsnip 'Pearl'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":true,"price":null},{"id":298135,"botanicalName":"<em>Pastinaca</em> <em>sativa</em> 'Picador'<sup>PBR</sup>","entityDescription":"A really vigorous variety that produces long, tapering, white roots with an excellent flavour. Good resistance to canker.  Sow: March to May, Harvest: October to February","image":"ParsnipPicador.jpg","commonName":"parsnip 'Picodor'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":true,"price":null},{"id":379034,"botanicalName":"<em>Pastinaca</em> <em>sativa</em> 'Sabre'","entityDescription":"Introduced to the market in 2021, this hybrid parsnip has so far received good reviews. It is being touted as a good variety to grow for the show bench due to its smooth, white skin and its long roots (rumours are that it can grow to 5ft long if grown in barrels). As well as being good to look at, this variety has a sweet flavour and a good resistance to canker.  Sow: February / March until May, Harvest: October to February","image":"","commonName":"parsnip 'Sabre'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":