me":"<em>Zantedeschia</em> 'Starry Night'<sup>PBR</sup>","entityDescription":"A clump-forming, tuberous perennial with broadly lance-shaped, white-flecked dark green leaves with deep purplish-black leaf margins and flushed with dark purple on the undersides. Dark purple almost black flowers are borne on deep purple stems in summer","image":"Web_Use-_KOS4513_12482.jpg","commonName":"arum lily 'Starry Night'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":341964,"botanicalName":"<em>Zantedeschia</em> 'White Flirt'<sup>PBR</sup>","entityDescription":"A clump-forming, tuberous perennial to 55cm tall with dark green, broadly lance-shaped leaves flecked and spotted with white. Flowers comprise an outer white spathe flushed cream towards the base surrounding the inner pale yellow spadix","image":"Web_Use-_KOS5326_12473.jpg","commonName":"arum lily 'White Flirt'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":355316,"botanicalName":"<em>Zantedeschia</em> 'Yelloween'","entityDescription":"A clump-forming, tuberous perennial to around 60cm tall with olive to mid-green, wavy, arrow- to triangular-shaped leaves marked with white spots. Bright yellow flowers are borne on strong stems in summer","image":"Web_Use-_KOS5423_12466.jpg","commonName":"arum lily 'Yelloween'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":500596,"botanicalName":"<em>Zantedeschia</em> <span style=\"font-variant: small-caps;\">Dubai Nights</span> ('Dozanduni'<sup>PBR</sup>)","entityDescription":"A compact, tuberous perennial with elongated heart-shaped white-flecked, dark to mid-green leaves. In summer,rich velvety-purple hood-shaped flowers or spathes, with acreamy-white border and edging, and a central yellow spadix, are produced on upright stems","image":"Web_Use-B21A6513_12187.jpg","commonName":"arum lily 'Dubai Nights'","nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":19191,"botanicalName":"<em>Zantedeschia</em> <em>elliottiana</em>","entityDescription":"A rhizomatous perennial to 90cm, with arrow-shaped dark green leaves finely spotted with white, and yellow spathes to 15cm long","image":"visi37138.jpg","commonName":"golden calla","nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":500352,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> 'Mirai 003Y'","entityDescription":"A ‘Supersweet’ early variety which produces slightly shorter cobs, at approximately 15cm long, but the shorter length is made up for by the really sweet juicy kernels (some eat them raw they’re so sweet). Each plant also usually produces at least two cobs and the cobs are filled well to the end. This variety is fairly vigorous so is a good one to try in poorer soil conditions.  Sow: April to May.  Sow / plant outside: May.  Harvest: August to September","image":"","commonName":"sweet corn [Mirai Picnic]","nurseriesCount":2,"synonymParentPlantId":353670,"synonymParentPlantName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> <span style=\"font-variant: small-caps;\">Mirai Picnic</span> ('Mirai 003Y')","isAgm":true,"isSynonym":true,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":105543,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em>","entityDescription":"Sweetcorn is relatively easy to grow and without a doubt tastes so much better that shop bought. There's a great range of varieties to grow, both heritage and modern,  and something to suit most gardens and the shorter British summers. If you are short on space you could also try growing baby corn.  Sweetcorn is generally classified into three groups.  'Normal' which is the original type of sweetcorn and seeds tend to have the most vigour - they're not as sweet as some of the newer varieties and should be eaten as soon as picked to avoid the sugar being converted to starch.  'Supersweet' which, as the name suggests, have a high sugar content but are not so easy to germinate. 'Supersweet' varieties should not be grown with other varieties as cross-pollination can result in a reduction in sugar content. 'Tendersweet' which are both tender and sweet (although not as sweet as 'supersweet') and can be grown alongside other varieties.","image":"visi04134.jpg","commonName":"sweet corn","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":293103,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Conqueror'","entityDescription":"A sweetcorn variety that can cope with the British summer weather is surely one to try! This is a ‘Tendersweet’ variety producing 2 -3 long cobs per plant, mid to late season. Each cob is full to the tip, has a fabulous flavour and look attractive. Plants are tall so may need staking.  Sow: April to May.  Sow / plant outside: May.  Harvest: September to October","image":"","commonName":"sweet corn 'Conqueror'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":379083,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Double Red'","entityDescription":"This variety is beautiful – the plant itself has red stalks and husks and the male flowers, or tassels, are also tinted red. The sweetcorn kernels are white and red (sometimes almost purple), and the colour distribution may vary across cobs. The taste is sweet and delicious when fresh, but it can also be dried for cornmeal and flour. This is one of the few red sweetcorns that matures quickly enough in the short British summers so enjoy some new colour in your veg patch! Expect good yields from short stocky plants.\r\nMatures in 80-100 days.","image":"","commonName":"sweetcorn 'Double Red'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":209818,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Earlibird'","entityDescription":"This is the earliest of the ‘Supersweet’ varieties and so great to grow during the British summers. Produces one to two cobs per plant , each of which is approximately 20cm long and super, super sweet! This variety has a good tolerance to cold soil which helps with germination if you are planting ‘in situ’.  As with other Supersweet varieties it is best not to grow this with other varieties.  Sow: April to May.  Sow / plant outside: May.  Harvest: August to September","image":"","commonName":"sweet corn 'Earlibird' (supersweet)","nurseriesCount":3,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":353667,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Goldcrest'","entityDescription":"This ‘Supersweet’ variety produces two well-filled, delicious cobs per plant. It is reliable, vigorous and performs well in the British summer, although may need some support in very windy conditions.  Sow indoors: March to May.  Sow / plant outside: May.  Harvest: August to October","image":"","commonName":"sweet corn 'Goldcrest'","nurseriesCount":2,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":120586,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Golden Bantam'","entityDescription":"This heritage variety was introduced in 1902 and produces sweet and tender cobs with kernels that are slightly larger than the more modern varieties. It will happily grow in cooler conditions and is both vigorous and early, ensuring it is still a popular variety today. \r\nSow indoors: April to May.  Sow / plant outdoors: May to June.  Harvest: August to October","image":"","commonName":"sweet corn 'Golden Bantam'","nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":209820,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Lark'","entityDescription":"An extra tender ‘tendersweet’ variety with sweet, soft, delicious cobs. It’s an early to mid-season variety, produces good yields and has good cold soil performance with more reliable germination than most of the supersweet varieties.  Sow indoors: April to May.  Sow / plant outside: May to June.  Harvest: August to September","image":"SweetcornLark.jpg","commonName":"sweet corn 'Lark'","nurseriesCount":2,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":209821,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Mainstay'","entityDescription":"A strong, midseason variety of sweetcorn producing large cobs that mature from late summer","image":"","commonName":"sweet corn 'Mainstay'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":293111,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Mirai 003'","entityDescription":"A ‘Supersweet’ early variety which produces slightly shorter cobs at approximately 15cm long but the shorter length is made up for by the really sweet juicy kernels (some eat them raw they’re so sweet). Each plant also usually produces at least two cobs and the cobs are filled well to the end.  Sow indoors: April to May.  Sow / plant outdoors: May to June.  Harvest: August to September","image":"","commonName":"sweet corn 'Mirai 003'","nurseriesCount":1,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":353669,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Mirai Gold'","entityDescription":"A ‘Supersweet’ variety which is extra tender and produces cobs up to 20cm long.  Tasty and attractive.  Sow indoors: April to May.  Sow / plant outdoors: May.  Harvest: August to October","image":"","commonName":"sweet corn 'Mirai Gold'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":117623,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Ovation'","entityDescription":"This early to mid-season 'supersweet' variety produces large cobs of 20 to 23 cm long, filled to the tip with delicious sweet kernels. It’s vigorous and consistent so looks impressive in the allotment.  Sow indoors: April to May.  Sow / plant outdoors: May.  Harvest: August to September","image":"SweetcornOvation.jpg","commonName":"sweet corn 'Ovation'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":true,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":293106,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Sparrow'","entityDescription":"A fast-growing annual which produces tender, well-filled kernels of yellow corn-on-the-cob. The plants grow to 1 - 1.5m with sword-shaped green leaves up the stem. The cobs form from female flowers in the axils of the lower leaves, whilst the male flowers form at the top. The cobs are ready to harvest August-September","image":"SweetcornSparrow.jpg","commonName":"sweet corn 'Sparrow'","nurseriesCount":0,"synonymParentPlantId":0,"synonymParentPlantName":null,"isAgm":false,"isSynonym":false,"isPlantsForPollinators":false,"price":null},{"id":209824,"botanicalName":"<em>Zea</em> <em>mays</em> 'Swift'","entityDescription":"This popular, quick growing 'extra tender-sweet' variety is well adapted to British conditions, producing one to two, well filled 20cm long cobs per plant, from late summer","image":"WSYD0010489.jpg","commonName":"sweet c